## Statement

Assume the complex flag-variety setup, including the Axiom of Choice, of
[[def-pa36-flag-tdo-and-module]]. On $Z=\mathbb P^1$ put
$L=\mathcal O_Z(-1)$, $D=\operatorname{Diff}(\mathcal O_Z)$ and
$D_0=\operatorname{Diff}(L)$. Form $E=D_0\otimes_{\mathcal O_Z}L$, using
the right $\mathcal O_Z$-action on $D_0$. Evaluation gives
$$E/D_0\Gamma(Z,E)\cong L.$$
Every quasi-coherent $D_0$-module $Q$ with $\Gamma(Z,Q)=0$ has the canonical
form
$$Q\cong L\otimes_{\mathbb C}\Gamma(Z,L^{-1}\otimes Q),$$
with trivial $\mathfrak{sl}_2$-action on the second factor.

For any commutative $\mathbb C$-algebra $R$, the same classification holds
for quasi-coherent modules over the vertical ring $D_{0,R}$ on
$\mathbb P^1_R$: its kernel modules are exactly
$L_R\otimes_R N$ for $R$-modules $N$. This form commutes with every scalar
extension $R\to R'$. If $R$ is noetherian, coherence over $D_{0,R}$ is
equivalent to finite generation of $N$ over $R$. Scalar extension here
concerns only the vertical ring and a central base ring; it makes no claim
about transporting arbitrary base derivations.

More generally let $U$ be a smooth affine complex variety and let $A$ be a
TDO on $U$. For the projection $r:Z\times U\to U$, a coherent
$(D_0\boxtimes A)$-module $Q$ satisfies $R^q r_*Q=0$ for $q>0$; moreover
$r_*Q=0$ if and only if the evaluation isomorphism is
$$Q\cong L\boxtimes N,\qquad N=r_*(L^{-1}\otimes Q),$$
where $N$ is a unique coherent $A$-module. Here the ordinary differential
inverse image has underlying sheaf $\mathcal O_Z\boxtimes N$ and relative
vector fields differentiate its $\mathcal O_Z$ factor. These isomorphisms
are natural in all commuting base differential operators. Consequently they
glue over locally trivial $\mathbb P^1$-bundles with compatible product TDO
charts and line bundle restricting to $L$, as in
[[lem-pa36-partial-flag-twist-product]].

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** The complex objects and modules in the statement; the relative
coherence assertion assumes coherence over the product differential ring,
not coherence over $D_0$ after forgetting the base.

[F1] Tensoring by $L$ identifies $D$-modules and $D_0$-modules; filtered
local differential operators have the PBW basis
([[lem-pa36-flag-tdo-pbw-and-twist]]).

[F2] For $Z$, both singular parameter $0$ and regular parameter $-\rho$
are antidominant. Global sections is exact on their quasi-coherent
D-modules, and at $-\rho$ localization and global sections are inverse
([[thm-pa36-flag-d-affinity]],
[[thm-pa36-regular-beilinson-bernstein-localization]]).

[F3] $H^0(Z,L)=H^1(Z,L)=0$, $H^0(Z,\mathcal O_Z)=\mathbb C$, and
$L\otimes T_Z=\mathcal O_Z(1)$ is generated by its two standard global
sections ([[lem-pa36-p1-line-bundle-cohomology]]).

[F4] An affine morphism has no positive higher direct images of
quasi-coherent sheaves
([[thm-affine-morphism-higher-direct-images-qc-vanish]]). The product charts
and their TDO transitions are those of
[[lem-pa36-partial-flag-twist-product]], with ordinary differential inverse
image $\mathcal O_Z\boxtimes N$.

## Proof

1.1 Morita untwisting identifies $E$ with $L\otimes D$. Its evaluation map $\epsilon:E\to L$ sends $\ell\otimes P$ to $\ell P(1)$. In an affine coordinate $x$, the unique normal form $P=\sum_{j\ge0}a_j\partial_x^j$ shows that $K=\ker\epsilon$ is locally $L\otimes D\partial_x$. Its order-one part is canonically $L\otimes T_Z$: a vector field intrinsically annihilates $1$, so this identification does not choose a connection or coordinate. PBW shows $K=D_0K_{\le1}$. By [F3] the two global sections of $K_{\le1}=\mathcal O_Z(1)$ generate $K$. Conversely every global section of $E$ maps to zero because $\Gamma L=0$. Thus $D_0\Gamma E=K$, proving the universal quotient. [F1, F3, algebra]

1.2 Let $q:Z\times U\to Z$ be the other projection. It is affine, so $q_*Q$ is quasi-coherent, carries the relative $D_0$-action, and has no positive higher direct images by [F4]. Leray and singular vanishing [F2] give $H^i(Z\times U,Q)=0$ for $i>0$. Apply the same argument over every affine open subset of $U$; the sheafification of these cohomology groups is $R^i r_*Q$, proving its vanishing. In particular $r_*$ is exact on quasi-coherent product differential modules. [F2, F4, algebra]

2.1 Put $T=L^{-1}\otimes Q$. By the regular equivalence in [F2], $T$ is generated over $D$ by its global sections, so $E^{(I)}\twoheadrightarrow Q$ for some set $I$. Since $\Gamma Q=0$, each summand kills $\Gamma E$ and hence $D_0\Gamma E$. Step 1.1 gives $L^{(I)}\twoheadrightarrow Q$, or $\mathcal O_Z^{(I)}\twoheadrightarrow T$. The kernel $H$ is a quasi-coherent $D$-module. Exactness of $\Gamma$ embeds $\Gamma H$ into $\mathbb C^{(I)}$; its $\mathfrak{sl}_2$-action is trivial. The natural map $\mathcal O_Z\otimes_{\mathbb C}\Gamma H\to H$ sends a constant horizontal section to that same section, and is $D$-linear because $H\subseteq\mathcal O_Z^{(I)}$. Its map on global sections is the identity of $\Gamma H$. Exactness and regular conservativity in [F2] kill its kernel and cokernel, so it is an isomorphism. Thus $H=\mathcal O_Z\otimes\Gamma H$ and $T=\mathcal O_Z\otimes V$, with $V=\Gamma T$. Untwisting gives the claimed canonical evaluation isomorphism and trivial action. The converse follows from $\Gamma L=0$. [F1, F2, F3, step 1.1, algebra]

3.1 For any central base ring $R$, push a module on $Z_R=Z\times\operatorname{Spec}R$ along the affine map to $Z$ and apply step 2.1. Multiplication by $R$ commutes with all vertical operators, so its natural evaluation is $R$-linear and reconstructs the sheaf as $L_R\otimes_R N$. Conversely this form has zero direct image by the Čech complex for $L$ tensored over $\mathbb C$ with $N$. Tensoring this form over $R$ with $R'$ proves the asserted scalar-extension compatibility without assuming flatness. If $R$ is noetherian, finite relative differential generators involve finitely many coefficients in $N$; these coefficients generate $N$ since vertical operators create no new coefficients. Conversely finitely generated $N$ gives a finite relative differential generating set, and PBW over the noetherian ring $R$ makes the relative differential ring left noetherian, proving coherence. [F1, F3, F4, step 2.1, algebra]

3.2 Suppose $r_*Q=0$. Then $\Gamma(Z,q_*Q)=\Gamma(Z\times U,Q)=0$. Step 2.1 applied to $q_*Q$ yields its canonical form $L\otimes V$. Multiplication by $\Gamma(U,\mathcal O_U)$ and every operator in $\Gamma(U,A)$ commute with the relative $D_0$ action, so naturality of step 2.1 makes $V$ a module over that base differential ring. Affine quasi-coherent sheafification identifies its sheaf with $N=r_*(L^{-1}\otimes Q)$ and the obtained isomorphism with $Q=L\boxtimes N$. No tensor product is taken over a noncommutative ring: the exterior product is over $\mathbb C$ with the two commuting actions. Conversely $r_*(L\boxtimes N)=0$ follows by the two-affine-open Čech calculation for $L$ tensored over $\mathbb C$ with $N$. [F3, F4, step 2.1, step 1.2, algebra]

4.1 The $A$-module $N$ of step 3.2 is coherent. On each of the two affine fiber charts choose finitely many product-differential generators of $Q$. This is possible for a coherent differential module on an affine smooth chart: its quasi-coherent underlying module is determined by sections; local finite generators on a finite principal-open cover become global generators after clearing denominators, and differential operators localize by the Leibniz rule. Trivialize $L$ there and express each chosen generator as a finite sum of polynomials in the fiber coordinate times sections of $N$. Let $N_0$ be the base differential submodule generated by the finitely many coefficients from both charts. Product differential operators preserve $L\boxtimes N_0$, so the chosen generators imply $L\boxtimes N_0=L\boxtimes N$. Evaluation at any fiber point gives $N_0=N$, proving finite generation. A TDO on a smooth affine chart is left noetherian: PBW gives the noetherian graded algebra $\operatorname{Sym}T_U$; lift finitely many graded generators and induct on order to generate any left ideal. Thus finite generation implies coherence. [F1, step 3.2, algebra]

5.1 Finally the map $p^\dagger p_*(L^{-1}\otimes Q)\to L^{-1}\otimes Q$ is the intrinsic differential evaluation map. On each product chart it is the isomorphism of step 3.2. A change of torsor section acts on the fiber coordinate by its transition, and the chain rule intertwines relative differentiation; the base differential action transforms by the given TDO cocycle. Evaluation therefore agrees on overlaps and glues. Moreover $p_*p^\dagger N=N$, since $p_*\mathcal O=\mathcal O$ on a $\mathbb P^1$-bundle, so $N$ is unique up to its canonical isomorphism. The argument uses choice only for the permitted bases and generating sets in [F2] and the inherited suppliers. [F3, F4, step 3.2, step 4.1, algebra] ∎

